    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Strong Indian MBA programs produced average fresh graduate CTCs well above pre-MBA salaries in
    the most recent placement cycles, and most graduates who choose a specialization matching their
    background and institute strength recover the cost within two to four years. But that math
    describes strong programs and reasonably strong candidates, not every MBA everywhere. A
    specialization chosen for the wrong institute, or an institute chosen purely for a specialization
    label, both weaken this math badly.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>A useful planning check, not a universal rule:</strong> treat roughly 10% of your
      family's total realistic education budget as the strict default ceiling for a single MBA,
      specialization included. A Rs 20-25 lakh Tier-1 IIM fee is only worth spending materially above
      that ceiling when you can point to course-specific evidence, a verified placement report for
      your target specialization at that exact institute, real faculty and recruiter depth in that
      function, and a fee-to-CTC break-even you can actually run the numbers on, not the institute's
      brand name or ranking alone. The same 10% of budget spent on a lower-cost government or state
      MBA with real placement support, plus one proof-of-skill project, often moves a candidate toward
      a real, high-value skill portfolio and <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong> just as
      reliably as an expensive brand name with a weak specialization fit.
    </p>
  </div>

